WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.340
The 1964 Alaska earthquake was a very large subduction-related event, reported

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.780
as moment magnitude 9.2 in the course sources.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.220
It began beneath southern Alaska and ruptured a broad megathrust surface.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.920
The downgoing Pacific plate moved beneath the overriding North American plate.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.740
The textbook cross section shows a gently dipping interface, not a

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.500
continental rift or a purely strike-slip boundary.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.820
The aftershock distribution helped constrain the large affected fault area.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.020
The felt region and the region of severe property damage were

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:32.680
different sizes.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.160
Shaking damaged Anchorage and triggered major landslides.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.940
Weak ground and slope failure caused buildings and neighborhoods to settle

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.540
or collapse. Some coastal areas rose while others subsided.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.520
Uplift exposed marine organisms above their former tide level;

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.960
subsidence allowed seawater to flood former dry land.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:58.460
Seafloor displacement generated a Pacific tsunami, while local submarine landslides produced

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.840
additional waves. Do not assume every local wave height represents the

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:03.680
same source mechanism.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.560
Surveyors mapped uplift and subsidence rather than assuming a uniform elevation

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.920
change. The coherent regional pattern supported thrusting at a convergent margin.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.360
This work helped establish the subduction interpretation during the development of

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.860
plate tectonics. The nearby volcanic arc provides an additional regional clue

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.760
to subduction. The map’s broad boundary of ground and ice cracking

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.800
marks an affected region; it must not be read as one

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.460
continuous fault fissure thousands of kilometers long.
